01-genesis/18.tex-%	\footnote{
01-genesis/18.tex-%		My Lord. Why does Abraham run to the three people and bow to them but
01-genesis/18.tex-%		then speak to God? Is he meeting God, people, or angels? Several of the
01-genesis/18.tex-%		biblical stories involving angels contain confusions such as this, i.e.,
01-genesis/18.tex-%		confusions between when it is the deity and when it is the angel who is
01-genesis/18.tex-%		speaking or doing something. But it is confusing only so long as we
01-genesis/18.tex-%		imagine angels as beings who are independent or separate from God. These
01-genesis/18.tex-%		texts indicate that angels are rather conceived of here as expressions
01-genesis/18.tex-%		of God’s presence. The consistent biblical conception of God is that God
01-genesis/18.tex-%		cannot possibly be seen by a human (“A human will not see me and live,”
01-genesis/18.tex-%		Exod 33:20) and cannot possibly be contained in any known space (“The
01-genesis/18.tex-%		heavens … will not contain you,” 1 Kings 8:27). God, in this conception,
01-genesis/18.tex-%		can nonetheless make Himself known to humans by a sort of emanation from
01-genesis/18.tex-%		the Godhead that is visible to human eyes. It is a hypostasis, a
01-genesis/18.tex-%		concrete expression of the divine presence, which is otherwise
01-genesis/18.tex-%		inexpressible to human beings. What the human sees when such a
01-genesis/18.tex-%		hypostasis is in front of him or her looks like “people,” like a “man.”
01-genesis/18.tex-%		And the word for such a thing is “angel.” Thus Jacob can encounter an
01-genesis/18.tex-%		angel and still say “I’ve seen God face-to-face” (Gen 32:31). And
01-genesis/18.tex-%		Abraham can face three angels and address “my Lord.” And an angel can
01-genesis/18.tex-%		speak God’s words in first person or can speak about God in third
01-genesis/18.tex-%		person. All this is so because in some ways an angel is an identifiable
01-genesis/18.tex-%		thing itself, and in some ways it is merely a representation of divine
01-genesis/18.tex-%		presence in human affairs. (See The Hidden Face of God, pp. 10–11.)
01-genesis/18.tex-%	}
