01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	And he%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fB{I can't even with this.}
01-genesis/32.tex-	touched the inside of his%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fB{Red can't handle this.}
01-genesis/32.tex-	thigh%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fA{
01-genesis/32.tex-		The usual translation, ``thigh,'' understates the range of the
01-genesis/32.tex-		term \heb{ירך}. Across its 34 occurrences (32 verses) in the Masoretic Text,
01-genesis/32.tex-		it means thigh/hip/haunch (Gen 32:26--33; Judg 3:16,21; Ps 45:4),
01-genesis/32.tex-		groin/loins (Gen 24:2,9; 47:29; Exod 28:42),
01-genesis/32.tex-		reproductive source---descendants literally ``come out of'' one's
01-genesis/32.tex-		\heb{ירך} (Gen 46:26; Exod 1:5; Judg 8:30)---and, spatially, the side/flank
01-genesis/32.tex-		of the tabernacle or altar (Exod 40:22,24; Lev 1:11; Num 3:29,35)
01-genesis/32.tex-		and base/shaft of the menorah (Exod 25:31; 37:17; Num 8:4).
01-genesis/32.tex-		Num 5:21--27 likewise uses it for the woman's reproductive/groin region;
01-genesis/32.tex-		Song 7:2 for the curves of the thighs/hips;
01-genesis/32.tex-		Jer 31:19 and Ezek 21:17 for striking the thigh in distress;
01-genesis/32.tex-		Ezek 24:4 for an animal haunch; and Judg 15:8 in the idiom
01-genesis/32.tex-		\heb{שוק על ירך}. Thus the range---thigh/hip/haunch $\rightarrow$
01-genesis/32.tex-		groin/loins/reproductive source, and side/flank $\rightarrow$ base---is
01-genesis/32.tex-		directly attested in Biblical Hebrew, not merely reconstructed from
01-genesis/32.tex-		cognates. Comparative caution: Phoenician KAI 4's \textit{yrk} is the
01-genesis/32.tex-		verb ``prolong,'' not this noun; Akkadian likewise has no simple
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{yarku} ``thigh,'' though \textit{warkatu/urkatu} means
01-genesis/32.tex-		rear/backside/buttocks (while words such as \textit{qablu} can denote
01-genesis/32.tex-		loins/hips). Consequently Gen 24:2,9 and 47:29's ``place your hand under my
01-genesis/32.tex-		\heb{ירך}'' can carry much stronger groin/loins/reproductive force than
01-genesis/32.tex-		``under my thigh.'' Gen 32:26--33's \heb{כף ירך}, typically translated
01-genesis/32.tex-		as thigh, is expressed with the same word elsewhere used for
01-genesis/32.tex-		the reproductive loins. The lexical connection between the oath scenes
01-genesis/32.tex-		and Jacob's injury is therefore considerably more suggestive than the
01-genesis/32.tex-		English gloss ``thigh'' makes apparent.
01-genesis/32.tex-	}
01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	And the inside of Jacob’s thigh is dislocated.%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fC{
01-genesis/32.tex-		In Gen.~32:26, ``dislocated'' is \textit{watt\=eqa`} (\heb{ותקע}),
01-genesis/32.tex-		from \textit{t-q-`} (\heb{תקע}), a remarkably broad verb meaning
01-genesis/32.tex-		drive/thrust in, fix or fasten, strike, clap, or blow a horn;
01-genesis/32.tex-		``dislocate'' here is inferred from context. It is also the verb used
01-genesis/32.tex-		throughout Josh.~6 for what the seven priests do with their horns as
01-genesis/32.tex-		Israel circles Jericho for seven days, with the rear guard following the
01-genesis/32.tex-		Ark. Most suggestively, the same verb occurs in Num.~25:4 for what is to
01-genesis/32.tex-		be done to the Israelite leaders after the Baal-Peor apostasy---an episode
01-genesis/32.tex-		associated with a fertility cult and explicitly with sexual relations
01-genesis/32.tex-		between Israelite men and Moabite women---where its disputed sense may be
01-genesis/32.tex-		``hang,'' ``impale,'' or ``expose.'' The underlying physical range is thus
01-genesis/32.tex-		something like forcefully drive, strike, fix, or leave out of position;
01-genesis/32.tex-		Gen.~32:26's specific sense ``wrench/dislocate'' is possible, but hardly
01-genesis/32.tex-		lexically inevitable.
01-genesis/32.tex-	}
