01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	He\fB{Who?!} says ``I won't let you go until you kneel.''%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fC{
01-genesis/32.tex-		``Bless'' is \textit{b-r-k} (\heb{ברך}), the same consonantal root as
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{berek}, knee.'' The root can also mean to kneel or bow, and the
01-genesis/32.tex-		plural ``knees'' can mean the lap---the space formed by the knees when
01-genesis/32.tex-		sitting. Thus the semantic cloud around \textit{b-r-k} includes
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{blessing}, \textit{kneeling/bowing}, \textit{knee}, and
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{lap}. The overlap is especially suggestive here: Jacob demands a
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{b-r-k}, a blessing, in a scene already obsessively concerned with
01-genesis/32.tex-		his thigh, hip, and groin---and the same three consonants also point toward
01-genesis/32.tex-		kneeling and the lap.
01-genesis/32.tex-	}
