01-genesis/32.tex-{
01-genesis/32.tex-	So Jacob asks.\fC{
01-genesis/32.tex-		The verb here is \heb{שאל} (\textit{š-ʾ-l}), conventionally ``ask,'' but
01-genesis/32.tex-		its semantic range is much wider. Biblical Hebrew uses the root for
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{ask, question, request, petition, seek, desire, demand, beg}.
01-genesis/32.tex-		One can \heb{שאל} for information, for an object or favor,
01-genesis/32.tex-		for permission, for someone's welfare, or for an answer, favor, or
01-genesis/32.tex-		decision from a deity or oracle. The intensity is therefore supplied
01-genesis/32.tex-		largely by context.	
01-genesis/32.tex-		
01-genesis/32.tex-		The same semantic center is ancient and widespread in Semitic. Ugaritic
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{šʾl} means to ask or inquire; Akkadian
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{šaʾālu}/\textit{šâlu} covers asking, questioning, inquiring,
01-genesis/32.tex-		consulting, asking for, and related petitionary uses; Aramaic and Syriac
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textit{šʾl} include asking, questioning, consulting, requesting,
01-genesis/32.tex-		entreating, and demanding; and cognates occur more widely in Semitic.
01-genesis/32.tex-		The common idea is not specifically ``asking a question,'' but
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textbf{seeking something from another by asking}---information, an
01-genesis/32.tex-		answer, an object, permission, assistance, or compliance.
01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-		The grammar of this verse is consequently worth preserving exactly.
01-genesis/32.tex-		It does \emph{not} say ``Jacob asked him.'' It says
01-genesis/32.tex-		\textbf{``and Jacob \heb{שאל}, and he said''}; the object of
01-genesis/32.tex-		\heb{שאל} is unstated. The following \textit{``Tell your name''}
01-genesis/32.tex-		makes a demand concerning the saying of some name of someone, but
01-genesis/32.tex-		``him'' is supplied by translators rather than expressed
01-genesis/32.tex-		by the Hebrew. Nor does the following \textit{``and he said''} identify
01-genesis/32.tex-		its subject by name: Hebrew simply continues with another masculine
01-genesis/32.tex-		singular ``he.''
01-genesis/32.tex-	
01-genesis/32.tex-		This matters especially in a passage whose masculine pronouns are already
01-genesis/32.tex-		remarkably slippery. The lexical range permits the first clause to be
01-genesis/32.tex-		heard in a variety of ways, without specifying in the verb
01-genesis/32.tex-		itself exactly what was sought or from whom. Only the ensuing speech
01-genesis/32.tex-		provides the contextual clue. The response then repeats the same root:
01-genesis/32.tex-		\heb{שאל}, conjugated here as \heb{ישאל}, which, curiously, is one
01-genesis/32.tex-		letter away from \heb{ישראל} (Israel).
01-genesis/32.tex-	}\fB{
01-genesis/32.tex-		Lit. ``Jacob begs'' in the original text.%
01-genesis/32.tex-	}
01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	He says ``What's your \paleo{שמ}?''
01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	He says ``Why ask\footnote{Ibid.} this \paleo{שמ}?''
01-genesis/32.tex-
01-genesis/32.tex-	He blesses\footnote{
01-genesis/32.tex-		Lit. \paleo{ברכ} in the original,
01-genesis/32.tex-		a verb meaning ``to kneel, bend the knee''
01-genesis/32.tex-		in the standard Qal form. The form
01-genesis/32.tex-		here is the Piel form, the intensified
01-genesis/32.tex-		form of the same verb, commonly
01-genesis/32.tex-		translated as ``bless.''
01-genesis/32.tex-	} him \paleo{שמ}.
