01-genesis/32.tex-{
01-genesis/32.tex-	\aB{Deliverance!}%
01-genesis/32.tex-	\fC{%
01-genesis/32.tex-		The root \heb{נ־צ־ל} has the basic sense of \textbf{taking away, stripping
01-genesis/32.tex-		away, or pulling something out from somewhere}. In the Niphal, as here,
01-genesis/32.tex-		it means \textbf{to be delivered, rescued, saved, or to survive}---literally
01-genesis/32.tex-		something like being \textit{pulled out} of danger. The same root develops
01-genesis/32.tex-		quite differently in the Piel: \textbf{to make use of, exploit, or abuse}
01-genesis/32.tex-		someone or something, presumably from the idea of \textit{taking something
01-genesis/32.tex-		out of} it. Thus ``my life has been delivered'' can retain a concrete
01-genesis/32.tex-		undertone: Jacob's life has been \textbf{pulled out intact} from his
01-genesis/32.tex-		face-to-face encounter with {\God}.
01-genesis/32.tex-	}
01-genesis/32.tex-}
