08-samuel/2-06.tex-\Verse{11}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\hJ{וַיֵּשֶׁב אֲרוֹן יְהֹוָה בֵּית עֹבֵד אֱדֹם הַגִּתִּי שְׁלֹשָׁה חֳדָשִׁים וַיְבָרֶךְ יְהֹוָה אֶת־עֹבֵד אֱדֹם וְאֶת־כּל־בֵּיתוֹ׃}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\eJ{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		The Ark of YHWH remained in the house of Obed-edom the Gittite
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		three months, and YHWH blessed Obed-edom and his whole household.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		The box stays there three months.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		It makes Servant Of Edom and his whole family have really good luck.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-\Verse{12}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\hJ{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		וַיֻּגַּד לַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד לֵאמֹר בֵּרַךְ יְהֹוָה אֶת־בֵּית עֹבֵד אֱדֹם וְאֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לוֹ בַּעֲבוּר אֲרוֹן הָאֱלֹהִים וַיֵּלֶךְ דָּוִד וַיַּעַל אֶת־אֲרוֹן הָאֱלֹהִים מִבֵּית
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		עֹבֵד אֱדֹם עִיר דָּוִד בְּשִׂמְחָה׃
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\eJ{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		It was reported to King David: “YHWH has blessed Obed-edom’s house
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		and all that belongs to him because of the Ark of God.” Thereupon
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		David went and brought up the Ark of God from the house of
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Obed-edom to the City of David, amid rejoicing.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		David hears that things are going great for Obed-edom because of the Box.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		So David changes his mind and goes and fetches the Box.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		He brings it to David City (aka, Jerusalem) and the people celebrate.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-\Verse{13}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\hJ{וַיְהִי כִּי צָעֲדוּ נֹשְׂאֵי אֲרוֹן־יְהֹוָה שִׁשָּׁה צְעָדִים וַיִּזְבַּח שׁוֹר וּמְרִיא׃}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\eJ{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		When the bearers of the Ark of YHWH had moved forward six paces,
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		he sacrificed an ox and a fatling.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Ok, it's not clear if Obed-edom lives in Gath or if he's just \textit{from} Gath, but by my calculations, Gath to Jerusalem is about a 13 hour walk.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Now we're not interested in time here, we're interested in steps.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		So assuming an average stride length, that's about 80,000 steps.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		On the other hand, if we assume Obed-edom's house is just a mile away from the palace, that's more like 2,000 steps.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Now, some quick background on the animals:
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Ox: An adult cow. Basically a farm tractor made of meat.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Fatling: This can be a young cow, or sometimes another livestock animal. Basically a grain-fed, premium steak animal raised for a special dinner.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Now, in terms of cost, as best as I can estimate, circa 1000 BC when this is all taking place, in terms of the hours worked at the average wage:
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		\begingroup
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		\scriptsize
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		\setlength{\tabcolsep}{0.35em}
08-samuel/2-06.tex:		\Table{|p{0.17\linewidth}|p{0.19\linewidth}|p{0.52\linewidth}|}{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					\hline
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					Animal
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& Approximate Price
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& Equivalent Labor \\
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					\hline
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					Young calf (fatling)
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& 5–10 shekels
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& 5–10 months (\~{}900–1,800 work hours) \\
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					\hline
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					Ox (trained working animal)
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& 20–30 shekels
08-samuel/2-06.tex-						& 20–30 months (\~{}3,600–5,400 work hours) \\
08-samuel/2-06.tex-					\hline
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		\endgroup
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Let's agree that ``1 ox + 1 fatling'' is about 30 shekels in 1000 BC, or about 30 months work at the average wage.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		As for weight, a conservative estimate is that a fatling weighs 500 lbs and the ox weighs 1000 lbs.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		That's all the background we need. Now let's get down to business.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		David wants the God Box, because it's lucky and he wants the luck.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		But he's clearly a bit freaked out by the ``Touch it and you die'' thing, as noted in 2 Sam 6:8-10.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Summary: in this scene, David isn't taking any risks.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		How do you make the gods happy?
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Naturally, sacrifices.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		So, the text notes that David kills an ox and a fatling every time the guys move six steps.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Now, in our more conservative estimate of the distance of house to palace, that's 2,000 steps, or 2000/6 = 333 adult cows and 333 young cows (or whatever animal that was) for a total of 666 dead animals weighing almost exactly half a million pounds, spread out over a mile long walk, at a cost of about 10,000 shekels total, or 10,000 months (833 years) of work at the average wage.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		However, if ``Obed-edom the Gath guy'' does in fact live in Gath, then we're looking at 80,000/6 = 13,333 sacrifices of 1,500 lbs each for a total of 20 million pounds of meat stretching over 32.3 miles in a very much not refrigerated outdoor mid-day sun, at a cost of 400,000 shekels total, or 400,000 months (33,333 years) of work at the average wage.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Now During the Davidic era (around 1000 BC), Jerusalem was a small hilltop fortress town.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		Most mainstream archaeologists and historians estimate the population of Jerusalem was roughly 1,500 to 2,000 people.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	\aA{
08-samuel/2-06.tex-		The estimated population of Israel overall during the Davidic period ranges drastically between 40,000 to 300,000 people according to archaeological estimates, compared to several million people implied by biblical texts.
08-samuel/2-06.tex-	}
08-samuel/2-06.tex-
